Task 1: Setting Process Priority with nice
1.1 Understanding Nice Values
Linux priority range: -20 (highest) to 19 (lowest)
Default nice value: 0
Lower nice value = higher priority
1.2 Launching a Test Process
dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/null &
This creates a CPU-intensive background process
1.3 Checking Current Priority
ps -l -p $(pgrep -n dd)
Look for the NI column in output
Expected: NI value should be 0 (default)
1.4 Starting Process with Custom Priority
nice -n 10 dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/null &
Starts dd with nice value 10 (lower priority)
1.5 Verification
ps -l -p $(pgrep -n dd)
Expected: Should show NI value of 10
Task 2: Adjusting Priority with renice
2.1 Identifying Running Process
pgrep -l dd
Note the PID of your dd process
2.2 Changing Priority of Running Process
sudo renice -n 15 -p <PID>
Replace <PID> with actual process ID

2.3 Verification
ps -l -p <PID>
Expected: NI value should now be 15
2.4 Increasing Priority (Requires Root)
sudo renice -n -5 -p <PID>
Sets higher priority (negative nice)
Task 3: Monitoring CPU Usage Effects
3.1 Launching Monitoring Tool
htop
Press F6 to sort by CPU% or NI
3.2 Observing Priority Effects
Note CPU% for processes with different nice values
Higher priority processes get more CPU time
3.3 Creating Priority Competition
nice -n 19 dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/null &
nice -n -5 dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/null &
Observe how they compete for CPU in htop
Troubleshooting Tips
If renice fails:
Verify PID exists: ps -p <PID>
Ensure you have sudo privileges
Processes may show 0% CPU if system isn't under load
Create load: stress --cpu 4
Cleanup
pkill dd
